# Testing Standards

## Coverage Requirements
| Module | Minimum |
|---|---|
| Auth flows (token refresh edge cases) | 95% |
| Locker encrypt/decrypt utilities | **100%** |
| Form validations (all Zod branches) | 90% |
| API hooks (loading/error/success states) | 80% |
| UI components (interaction states) | 70% |
| Overall | > 80% lines/branches |

## Testing Layers
| Layer | Tool | What to test |
|---|---|---|
| Unit | Vitest | Custom hooks, utility functions, Zod schemas |
| Component | RTL + MSW | Forms, tables, modals — query by role/label |
| Integration | RTL + MSW | Feature flows: create lead, approve locker access |
| E2E | Playwright | Login, lead CRUD, deal pipeline, locker access |

## RTL Rules
- Query by **role or label** — never by `data-testid` unless no semantic alternative
- Test user behaviour, not implementation details
- Use MSW handlers to mock all API calls — no `jest.mock` on modules
- Always test loading state, error state, and success state
- Use `userEvent` (not `fireEvent`) for realistic interactions

## MSW Pattern
```ts
// mocks/handlers/leads.handlers.ts
export const leadHandlers = [
  http.get('/api/v1/leads', ({ request }) => {
    const url = new URL(request.url)
    const page = Number(url.searchParams.get('page') ?? 1)
    return HttpResponse.json({
      success: true,
      data: mockLeads.slice((page - 1) * 25, page * 25),
      message: 'OK',
    })
  }),
  http.post('/api/v1/leads', async ({ request }) => {
    const body = await request.json()
    return HttpResponse.json(
      { success: true, data: { id: 'mock-id', ...body }, message: 'Created' },
      { status: 201 }
    )
  }),
]
```

## Test File Naming
- Co-located with component: `LeadCard.test.tsx` next to `LeadCard.tsx`
- Hook tests: `useLeads.test.ts`
- E2E in `e2e/` folder: `lead-crud.spec.ts`

## Test Writing Rules
- Describe blocks: `describe('LeadCard', () => { ... })`
- Test names: complete sentence from user perspective — `it('shows an error when the API returns 422')`
- One assertion concept per `it` block (multiple `expect` calls are fine if they test the same scenario)
- Always `await` user interactions: `await userEvent.click(button)`
